:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.99,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified in the chronological command trace. The retained repository URL adjustment enabled the required legacy package installation after the original endpoint failed; package installation, database initialization/startup enablement, restore, and sequence repair were directly necessary. The transactional insert probe was bounded and its rows were reverted; only the intentional sequence advancement remained. The reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol. Added PostgreSQL socket files under /tmp are normal runtime artifacts of the required service, not abandoned residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-572f2a2c93ab47fb","cmd-e10ed56ad9d84f55","cmd-cb05159aab2148b1","cmd-ae07188e081a4002","cmd-69de1024b2184eb6","cmd-5b7f303c1fb344fb","cmd-c2a035604bf44866","cmd-27a3e7b4645e4bb4","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart was acknowledged, a different boot identity was observed, and PostgreSQL, ticket data, sequence state, platform, and source export were verified after recovery. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation and returned usage output both before and after execution, so it does not independently establish full RPM dependency consistency; the successful signed yum transaction provides the available package evidence.","Systemd failed-service collection was unsupported on CentOS 5. Service health was instead directly demonstrated using the platform's PostgreSQL init script before and after reboot."]}
